# Build output. The archive is built, not shipped: nothing under build/ is tracked, and a
# consumer either links what it built here or compiles OOPS_SDK_C_SRCS itself.
build/
savedata/
*.o
*.a
*.elf

# There is deliberately no `!lib/*.a` negation here, and no `lib/` directory.
#
# The first version of this file carried one, and the README documented a slot for a vendor
# tiler archive beside it. Neither the directory nor the archive ever existed - but a
# documented slot plus a rule that un-ignores exactly the file that would fill it is how a
# vendor binary gets committed by somebody moving quickly. This repository links weak
# declarations against symbols the platform already exports; it does not carry the vendor's
# code, and there is nowhere for it to land. See OOPS conventions section 1.

# No `__pycache__/` or `*.pyc` rule, deliberately. There is no Python in this repository and
# there is not meant to be - the build is make and the scripts are shell. An ignore rule for
# bytecode is a quiet accommodation for a language that is not allowed here: it would hide the
# first stray file rather than let `git status` show it.

# Claude Code's per-person overrides. A `settings.json` beside it would be shared; the
# `.local` one is whoever is sitting here, and tracking it means their overrides arrive in
# everybody else's clone.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Whatever the person editing this happens to run. None of it is anybody else's business,
# and every one of these has been committed to some repository by accident at least once.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# **Probe and title output, which the SDK writes and nothing reads back.** `data/default/` is
# where a payload's own logging lands when it runs on the console, and a day of hardware runs
# leaves hundreds of files there. They are output, not work: committing them was nearly done
# once, and the reason to name them here rather than remember not to is that `git add -A` does
# not remember.
data/default/
